good afternoon and welcome back if you  were here this morning and welcome if  you just arrived for the afternoon  session I'm very pleased to introduce  Peter Schiffer elds vice provost for  research who'll give us a few thoughts  this afternoon on the topic of data and  data science peter is also a professor  of applied physics and physics here at  Yale he's a member of the American  Physical Society he's chair of the  division of material physics of the  society and past chair of the topical  group on magnetism in its applications  and if you don't know what that's about  talk to me later I'm sure I can explain  it probably no magic budget so without  further ado I'd like to welcome Peter  Schaffer all right Thank You Jill so it  is a genuine pleasure to be here and to  kick off the afternoon and I just want  to express first of all my gratitude to  everybody who has participated in the  organization of the event and putting  this together and all the speakers and  the panel members  this is really important for Yale and  I'm so glad that folks are helping put  it together and participating Yale is in  the midst of making a big push in  science and engineering it's an academic  priority and we are gearing up to make  it a reality this comes from president  salva is charged to us and it comes from  a sense of responsibility that Yale is  one of the great scholarly institutions  on the planet  has a responsibility to contribute in  science and engineering and there's a  sense of that from around campus sense  of that from our leadership and we're in  the midst of doing it and the  manifestation of that has been a long  process a couple year process of  strategic planning there was a wonderful  committee put together by the President  and provost led by Scott Strobel that  issued a very long report  90 odd pages I'm sure all of you have  read it in detail every one of you and  it it actually is a really really good  report and and it identified several  areas priority areas for increased  investment in the sciences and  engineering several cross-cutting themes  and several organizational structural  improvements that we should be making  and the reason I mention it not not just  because it's important to Yale as an  institution but also in this venue data  data science the structures that support  the use of data are prioritized in that  report more than anything else  data support is prioritized as an  organizational need the computational  resources to handle large data sets and  to analyze data are prioritized the  management of data are prioritized in  data science the fundamental mathematics  behind how we analyze data are  prioritized that's actually the first  among the top priority areas slated for  additional investment data are  incredibly important and I know I'm  preaching to the choir here but I just  want to emphasize that just like  everything else in society research is  being transformed by the availability of  massive sets of data it's it's different  from research that was conducted 10 20  30 years ago in the sense the the  quantity of data that's available the  fidelity of the data to reality and the  computational resources that we have our  hands that allow us to analyze the data  it's just it's a different world for  research and that goes across the full  spectrum of the scholarly enterprise  from the arts and humanities the social  sciences which oh you'll hear my  colleague Alan Gerber in a minute to all  of the Natural Sciences and engineering  every single field is being  pacted by the ability to collect or  gather or acquire large datasets and  then analyze them and that's wonderful  it allows us to do things that we  couldn't have dreamed of doing 10 20 30  years ago but it comes with a  responsibility because the people  federal government foundations  corporations that give us money to that  allows us to do our research they know  that we can use data now and so they  have higher expectations they have  expectations that we'll use it but also  expectations that we'll handle the data  appropriately all the privacy concerns  and the use concerns when it's  proprietary data and that we'll manage  it appropriately and this is something  that universities are learning how to do  is when we not just bring in data to use  but we create our own data where do we  put it and how do we store it and the  old model which you know I was raised in  where the PhD student would hand a hard  drive to the adviser upon graduating and  then wave goodbye and the the hard drive  would sit on the advisors shelf until  there were advisor moved offices and  then couldn't figure out what it was and  threw in the trash that model is no  longer acceptable it turns out so coming  up with the mechanisms to handle the  data that we create is going to be part  of what we do as well as having the  ability to bring in datasets and use  other people's data responsibly and  there's a lot of effort around that and  a lot of very high expectations from the  government and other funders for how we  do that fortunately at Yale we have  tremendous resources to help researchers  with that sitting right in front of me  and being in this building the library  is first and foremost among those but  also the Yale Center for research  computing I TS and the administrative  offices that help us deal with our  sponsors office of sponsored research  the also cooperative research on and on  and on we have lots of support here and  with the science priorities we're just  going to have to  building and building on that room that  support and it's gonna be the  responsibility of each one of us  involved in research enterprise to think  about how we acquire data how we use the  data and for the data we create what  happens to it when we're done and where  it where it goes and to make sure that's  in line with best practices so with that  let me say again thank you thank you to  everybody who helped with the stay  together thank you to those of you who  are here for the afternoon and to all  the speakers and unfortunately not gonna  able to stick around for the whole  afternoon aha but I'm really looking  forward to hearing what I can please  enjoy and do great things with data  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

